//! Collection types.
//!
//! navi's standard collection library provides efficient implementations of
//! the most common general purpose programming data structures, the same as
//! Rust's:
//!
//! - Sequences: [`Vec`], [`VecDeque`]
//! - Maps: [`HashMap`], [`BTreeMap`]
//! - Sets: [`HashSet`], [`BTreeSet`]
//! - Misc: [`BinaryHeap`]
//!
//! (Rust's `LinkedList` is not provided.)
//!
//! # When should you use which collection?
//!
//! Use a [`Vec`] when you want to collect items to process or send elsewhere
//! later, want a sequence in a particular order and only append to (or near)
//! the end, or want a stack or a resizable array.
//!
//! Use a [`VecDeque`] when you want a [`Vec`] that supports efficient
//! insertion at both ends of the sequence, or a queue.
//!
//! Use a [`HashMap`] when you want to associate arbitrary keys with an
//! arbitrary value, or a cache, or a map with no extra functionality.
//!
//! Use a [`BTreeMap`] when you want a map sorted by its keys, to be able to
//! get a range of entries on demand, or to find the smallest or largest
//! key-value pair.
//!
//! Use the `Set` variant of any of these `Map`s when you just want to remember
//! which keys you've seen, as there is no meaningful value to associate with
//! your keys.
//!
//! Use a [`BinaryHeap`] when you want to store a bunch of elements but only
//! ever want to process the "biggest" or "most important" one at any given
//! time, or a priority queue.
//!
//! # navi differences
//!
//! - All the collections are shared handles, as [`Vec`] is: assigning,
//!   passing or copying a collection shares its contents, so a change made
//!   through one copy is seen through the others. Use `clone()` for an
//!   independent copy.
//! - [`HashMap`] and [`HashSet`] hash with FxHash (fast, but not resistant to
//!   HashDoS attacks), and their iteration order is deterministic: the same
//!   insertions and removals give the same order on every run.
//! - [`BinaryHeap`] has no `peek_mut`, and its `into_sorted_vec` leaves the
//!   heap as it is; `drain` methods take the items out at once instead of
//!   lazily.
//! - Methods that take a closure take a `fn` type.
//!
//! # Examples
//!
//! Counting the words of a text with a [`HashMap`], then listing them sorted
//! with a [`BTreeMap`]:
//!
//! ```
//! use std::collections::{BTreeMap, HashMap};
//!
//! let text = "the cat saw the dog and the dog saw the cat";
//! let mut counts: HashMap<&str, i32> = HashMap::new();
//! for word in text.split_whitespace() {
//!     *counts.entry(word).or_insert(0) += 1;
//! }
//! assert_eq(counts[&"the"], 4);
//!
//! let sorted: BTreeMap<&str, i32> = counts.iter().map(|(k, v)| (*k, *v)).collect();
//! let words: Vec<&str> = sorted.keys().copied().collect();
//! assert_eq(words, Vec::from(["and", "cat", "dog", "saw", "the"]));
//! ```
//!
//! Collections share their contents when copied:
//!
//! ```
//! use std::collections::VecDeque;
//!
//! let mut queue: VecDeque<i32> = VecDeque::new();
//! let mut other = queue;
//! other.push_back(1);
//! assert_eq(queue.pop_front(), Some(1));
//!
//! let copy = other.clone();
//! other.push_back(2);
//! assert(copy.is_empty());
//! ```

pub mod binary_heap;
pub mod btree_map;
pub mod btree_set;
pub mod hash_map;
pub mod hash_set;
pub mod vec_deque;

pub use self::binary_heap::BinaryHeap;
pub use self::btree_map::BTreeMap;
pub use self::btree_set::BTreeSet;
pub use self::hash_map::HashMap;
pub use self::hash_set::HashSet;
pub use self::vec_deque::VecDeque;
